  "id": "Compound::DB01221 - Gene::116443",
  "verbalization": "Ketamine's interaction with Grin3a is supported by multiple pathways that converge on the NMDA glutamate receptor activity, a key mechanism in its pharmacological effects. The evidence suggests that Ketamine binds to Grin2d, Grin2c, and Grin2b, all of which are subunits of the NMDA receptor, and that these subunits participate in the regulation of NMDA receptor activity, which in turn is associated with Grin3a. This indicates a direct, mechanistic link between Ketamine and Grin3a through the NMDA receptor pathway. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust and reliable relationship, with the shared involvement of NMDA receptor activity as a central mediator. The presence of multiple binding sites on different Grin2 subunits further supports the idea that Ketamine modulates NMDA receptor function, potentially influencing Grin3a through these subunits. The integrated evidence highlights a clear, biologically plausible mechanism by which Ketamine may interact with Grin3a, emphasizing the role of NMDA receptor activity in this relationship.",
